{"title":"risk3sixty","url":"https://risk3sixty.com/blog","favicon":"https://risk3sixty.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/risk3sixty-favicon-3.png","elements":[{"title":"Hardware Hacking: Discovering a DoS in NetGear Router","contents":"<a class=\"elementor-post__thumbnail__link\" href=\"https://risk3sixty.com/blog/hardware-hacking-discovering-a-dos-in-netgear-router\" tabindex=\"-1\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-post__thumbnail\"><img width=\"300\" height=\"200\" src=\"https://risk3sixty.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/netgear-vuln-300x200.png\" class=\"attachment-medium size-medium wp-image-32017\" alt=\"\"></div>\n\t\t</a>\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-post__text\">\n\t\t\t\t<h3 class=\"elementor-post__title\">\n\t\t\t<a href=\"https://risk3sixty.com/blog/hardware-hacking-discovering-a-dos-in-netgear-router\">\n\t\t\t\tHardware Hacking: Discovering a DoS in NetGear Router\t\t\t</a>\n\t\t</h3>\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-post__excerpt\">\n\t\t\t<p>Our security team identified an unauthenticated Denial-of-Service (DoS) vulnerability affecting a Netgear router’s web management interface. During routine testing of an internet-connected device, we found that a flaw in the /goform/Unauth endpoint allows any network user to crash and reboot the router, disrupting downstream services. Despite early disclosure and multiple follow-ups, no guidance has been issued by Netgear as of their latest security updates. This post details the discovery process, failed firmware update attempts, hardware-level investigation, and the conditions under which the vulnerability can be triggered.</p>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-post__read-more-wrapper\">\n\t\t\n\t\t<a class=\"elementor-post__read-more\" href=\"https://risk3sixty.com/blog/hardware-hacking-discovering-a-dos-in-netgear-router\" aria-label=\"Read more about Hardware Hacking: Discovering a DoS in NetGear Router\" tabindex=\"-1\">\n\t\t\tRead More\t\t</a>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t</div>","link":"https://r